\title{Why Detecting Benchmark Contamination Is Harder Than You Think:\\Large-Scale Psychometric Evidence from 5,219 Language Models}

\author{Anonymous}

\date{}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

%% ============================================================
%% ABSTRACT
%% ============================================================
\begin{abstract}
Benchmark contamination threatens the validity of LLM evaluation, yet the community lacks rigorous understanding of \emph{why} existing detection methods fail and \emph{how hard} the problem truly is. We conduct the largest psychometric analysis of benchmark contamination to date, applying Item Response Theory (IRT) and Differential Item Functioning (DIF) analysis to the full METABENCH MMLU response matrix (5,219 models $\times$ 12,508 items). Our findings are predominantly negative---but informatively so. First, classical unidimensional IRT catastrophically misfits the full LLM population (RMSEA\,=\,0.14, CFI\,=\,0.72), and architecture stratification only partially remediates this (RMSEA\,=\,0.10 for decoder-only models). Second, DIF-based contamination flags are heavily confounded with capability growth: within-family analysis reveals that 62\% of cross-cohort flags also appear within model families where no differential contamination exists. Third, semi-synthetic validation with controlled ground truth shows detection power far below simulation predictions (TPR\,=\,0.41 vs.\ 0.72 at $\delta \geq 1.4$), quantifying a 43\% simulation-to-reality gap attributable to non-random missingness, ceiling/floor effects, and architecture clustering in real response data. We further show that alternative detectors (ConStat, DVD generation variance) and their ensemble fusion all fail to achieve reliable detection, with the best ensemble reaching only 0.48 TPR at $\delta \geq 1.4$. Rather than a detection solution, we contribute: (1) the first large-scale empirical evidence for \emph{why} classical psychometrics cannot be directly applied to LLM evaluation, (2) a confound-separation framework quantifying the entanglement between contamination and capability, and (3) a semi-synthetic validation benchmark providing controlled ground truth for evaluating any future contamination detector.
\end{abstract}

%% ============================================================
%% 1. INTRODUCTION
%% ============================================================
\section{Introduction}

LLM benchmarks implicitly assume temporal score comparability: when we state that ``Model B outperforms Model A on MMLU,'' we presume both scores reflect the same latent ability measured under equivalent conditions. Benchmark contamination---the inclusion of test items in training data---systematically erodes this validity assumption. The problem is compounded by the shift toward reinforcement learning (RL) training: Wang~\etal~(2026) demonstrate 14--20pp AUROC degradation for all reference-free detectors after approximately 156 GRPO steps, reducing most to near-random performance.

A natural hypothesis is that contamination can be recast as a \emph{psychometric} problem. In classical educational testing, Differential Item Functioning (DIF) detects items that behave differently across examinee groups after conditioning on overall ability---precisely the signature contamination should leave. If contaminated models memorize specific items, those items become differentially easier for exposed cohorts beyond what ability gains predict, creating detectable anomalies in Item Characteristic Curves. Crucially, DIF operates on group-level behavioral patterns, making it theoretically orthogonal to within-model output distribution smoothing induced by RL training.

This paper tests this hypothesis at unprecedented scale---and finds it wanting. Using the METABENCH dataset comprising 5,219 models evaluated on 12,508 MMLU items, we conduct the largest DIF analysis in LLM evaluation literature. Our investigation reveals three fundamental obstacles:

\textbf{(1) IRT model misfit.} The unidimensional ability assumption underlying classical IRT is severely violated in heterogeneous LLM populations. The full-population 2PL model yields RMSEA\,=\,0.14, far exceeding the 0.08 acceptability threshold. Even restricting to decoder-only architectures achieves only RMSEA\,=\,0.10. This misfit propagates to all downstream analyses, corrupting item parameter estimates and inflating false positive rates.

\textbf{(2) Contamination--capability confounding.} DIF analysis flags items as differentially functioning across temporal or ability-based cohorts, but capability growth produces the same signal. Within-family analysis (e.g., Llama-2$\to$3$\to$3.1, where the training pipeline is known) reveals 62\% overlap between cross-cohort and within-family DIF flags, indicating that DIF predominantly captures ``the model got better'' rather than ``the model was contaminated.''

\textbf{(3) Simulation-to-reality gap.} Semi-synthetic validation with injected contamination of known magnitude shows TPR\,=\,0.41 at $\delta \geq 1.4$---a 43\% gap from the 0.72 TPR predicted by idealized simulation. This gap arises from real-data noise structures (non-random missingness, ceiling/floor effects, architecture clustering) that no simulation captures.

Rather than presenting a failed detection system, we reframe these findings as the first large-scale empirical characterization of \emph{why} contamination detection is fundamentally harder than the community has assumed. We contribute a confound-separation framework, demonstrate that multiple detection paradigms (DIF, perturbation testing, generation variance) all fail for distinct but complementary reasons, and introduce a semi-synthetic validation benchmark that provides controlled ground truth superior to existing proxies like MMLU-CF ($\kappa = 0.07$ agreement with DIF) or GSM1K ($r = 0.14$ correlation).

%% ============================================================
%% 2. RELATED WORK
%% ============================================================
\section{Related Work}

\paragraph{Contamination detection.} Existing approaches include membership inference via output distributions \citep{shi2024detecting}, perturbation-based testing (ConStat; Dekoninck~\etal, 2024) that measures performance drops on semantically equivalent reformulations, and generation variance analysis (DVD; Duan~\etal, 2024) that identifies memorized items through reduced output diversity. All operate on within-model signals vulnerable to RL training. Growing Pains (Roberts~\etal, 2026) applies multidimensional IRT for extensible evaluation but addresses score comparability rather than contamination per se.

\paragraph{Benchmark regeneration.} LiveBench (White~\etal, 2024) addresses contamination through monthly item updates from recent sources but applies no psychometric calibration across versions. DyVal (Zhu~\etal, 2024) generates evaluation samples dynamically but cannot match difficulty distributions of established benchmarks.

\paragraph{IRT in NLP.} Prior work has applied IRT to NLP evaluation for item analysis \citep{lalor2019learning}, difficulty estimation, and examinee modeling. Ours is the first to systematically test IRT assumptions at the scale of thousands of models and to characterize failure modes specific to the LLM population.

%% ============================================================
%% 3. BACKGROUND AND METHOD
%% ============================================================
\section{Background and Method}

\subsection{Item Response Theory Preliminaries}

The two-parameter logistic (2PL) IRT model specifies the probability that examinee $j$ correctly answers item $i$ as:
\begin{equation}
P(X_{ij} = 1 \mid \theta_j) = \frac{1}{1 + \exp(-a_i(\theta_j - b_i))}
\end{equation}
where $\theta_j$ is the latent ability of model $j$, $a_i$ is item discrimination, and $b_i$ is item difficulty. The model assumes unidimensionality (a single latent trait) and local independence (responses are conditionally independent given $\theta$).

\subsection{Differential Item Functioning}

DIF occurs when examinees from different groups with equal ability have different probabilities of answering an item correctly. We employ the Mantel--Haenszel (MH) and logistic regression procedures, classifying items using the ETS A/B/C scheme where C-level flags indicate large DIF ($|\Delta_{\text{MH}}| > 1.5$).

We define two cohort structures: (a) \textbf{temporal cohorts} splitting models by release date (pre-2024 reference vs.\ 2024+ focal), and (b) \textbf{ability quintile cohorts} comparing the lowest and highest ability quintiles.

\subsection{Bifactor IRT Model}

To address multidimensionality, we fit a bifactor model:
\begin{equation}
P(X_{ij} = 1 \mid \theta_j, \eta_{jk}) = \frac{1}{1 + \exp(-(a_i^g \theta_j + a_i^{s_k} \eta_{jk} - d_i))}
\end{equation}
where $\theta_j$ is a general ability factor and $\eta_{jk}$ is a subject-specific factor for subject domain $k$.

\subsection{Semi-Synthetic Validation}

We inject controlled contamination into the real METABENCH response matrix by flipping responses for a designated ``focal'' group on selected items with effect size $\delta$ and exploitation rate $p$. This preserves real-data noise structures (missingness patterns, ceiling effects, architecture clustering) while providing item-level ground truth.

\subsection{Alternative Detectors}

We evaluate two alternative detection paradigms: (a) \textbf{ConStat-style perturbation testing}, measuring performance drops on 220 semantically equivalent MMLU reformulations, and (b) \textbf{DVD generation variance}, analyzing output diversity across 50 temperature-sampled responses per item for 13 models.

%% ============================================================
%% 4. EXPERIMENTS
%% ============================================================
\section{Experiments}

We organize our experiments around three diagnostic questions: (1) Does classical IRT adequately model the LLM population? (2) Can DIF---or any existing detector---reliably identify contamination? (3) Is psychometrically grounded benchmark regeneration feasible?

%% -------- 4.1 IRT Model Fit --------
\subsection{IRT Model Fit Diagnostics}
\label{sec:irt_fit}

We fit a 2PL IRT model to the full METABENCH MMLU response matrix (5,219 models $\times$ 12,508 items) and evaluate model fit using standard indices.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{IRT Model Fit Diagnostics. The full LLM population catastrophically violates the unidimensional assumption. Filtering negative-discrimination items and restricting to decoder-only architectures improve fit but remain at or above the 0.08 RMSEA acceptability threshold.}
\label{tab:irt_fit}
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Metric} & \textbf{Full Pop.} & \textbf{Neg-Disc Filtered} & \textbf{Decoder-Only} & \textbf{Dense Decoder} \\
\midrule
RMSEA & 0.14 & 0.11 & 0.10 & 0.08 \\
CFI & 0.72 & 0.79 & 0.82 & 0.88 \\
TLI & 0.69 & 0.76 & 0.79 & --- \\
SRMR & 0.09 & 0.07 & --- & --- \\
$N$ models & 5,219 & 5,219 & 3,420 & 1,850 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The full-population RMSEA of 0.14 far exceeds the conventional 0.08 threshold, and CFI\,=\,0.72 falls well below the 0.95 standard. The LLM population includes encoder--decoder, mixture-of-experts (MoE), and dense decoder-only architectures, each with fundamentally different response patterns that violate the unidimensional assumption. Approximately 15.4\% of items exhibit negative discrimination---items where higher-ability models are \emph{less} likely to answer correctly---further degrading fit.

\paragraph{Architecture stratification.} Restricting to decoder-only models ($N=3{,}420$) reduces RMSEA to 0.10 (Table~\ref{tab:arch_strat}). Further restriction to dense decoder-only ($N=1{,}850$) achieves RMSEA\,=\,0.08, the borderline-acceptable threshold, but at the cost of excluding 65\% of the population. MoE models remain poorly fit (RMSEA\,=\,0.12).

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Architecture Stratification Effect on IRT fit and DIF analysis. Restricting to homogeneous subpopulations improves fit but reduces sample size and does not eliminate within-family confounding.}
\label{tab:arch_strat}
\begin{tabular}{lcccccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Population} & $N$ & \textbf{RMSEA} & \textbf{CFI} & \textbf{DIF Rate} & \textbf{FPR} & \textbf{WF Overlap} \\
\midrule
Full population & 5,219 & 0.14 & 0.72 & 0.148 & 0.14 & 0.62 \\
Decoder-only & 3,420 & 0.10 & 0.82 & 0.120 & 0.11 & 0.55 \\
Dense decoder & 1,850 & 0.08 & 0.88 & 0.098 & 0.08 & 0.47 \\
MoE only & 380 & 0.12 & 0.78 & 0.135 & 0.13 & 0.58 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Effective detection power by architecture subgroup at $\delta \geq 1.4$. Homogeneous subgroups achieve better fit but lower absolute power due to reduced sample sizes.}
\label{tab:fit_breakdown}
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Subgroup} & \textbf{RMSEA} & $N$ & \textbf{Power at $\delta \geq 1.4$} \\
\midrule
All decoder & 0.10 & 3,420 & 0.45 \\
Dense small (1B--7B) & 0.07 & 1,200 & 0.38 \\
Dense large (13B--70B) & 0.09 & 650 & 0.42 \\
MoE all & 0.12 & 380 & 0.30 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

This creates a fundamental dilemma: adequate IRT fit requires restricting to homogeneous subpopulations, but this drastically reduces statistical power. The dense 1B--7B subgroup achieves the best fit (RMSEA\,=\,0.07) but only 0.38 power at $\delta \geq 1.4$.

%% -------- 4.2 DIF Detection & Confound --------
\subsection{DIF Detection and Contamination--Capability Confounding}
\label{sec:dif_confound}

We apply DIF analysis using two cohort definitions and examine the critical confound between contamination signals and capability growth.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{DIF Detection Summary by Cohort Definition. Different cohort strategies produce substantially different flag sets (41\% overlap), indicating high sensitivity to analytical choices.}
\label{tab:dif_summary}
\begin{tabular}{lccccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Cohort Type} & $N_\text{ref}$ & $N_\text{focal}$ & \textbf{DIF Flags} & \textbf{Flag Rate} & \textbf{Cross-Overlap} \\
\midrule
Temporal (2023 vs 2024) & 1,253 & 656 & 1,850 & 0.148 & 0.41 \\
Quintile (Q1 vs Q5) & 1,044 & 1,044 & 2,300 & 0.184 & 0.41 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Temporal cohort DIF flags 14.8\% of items, while quintile-based DIF flags 18.4\%. The two flag sets overlap only 41\%, demonstrating that DIF results are highly sensitive to cohort definition---a red flag for any method claimed as a reliable contamination detector.

\paragraph{Within-family confound analysis.} To disentangle contamination from capability growth, we examine DIF within model families sharing training pipelines (Table~\ref{tab:within_family}). If DIF genuinely detects contamination, flags should not appear within families where no differential contamination exists (\eg, Llama-2$\to$Llama-3 trained on the same pipeline with legitimate capability improvements).

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Within-Family Confound Analysis. A large fraction of cross-cohort DIF flags also appear within model families where no differential contamination exists, indicating DIF predominantly captures capability growth rather than contamination.}
\label{tab:within_family}
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Family} & $N_\text{models}$ & \textbf{Cross-Cohort Flags} & \textbf{Within-Family Flags} & \textbf{Overlap} \\
\midrule
Llama-2/3/3.1 & 47 & 312 & 213 & 0.68 \\
Qwen-1.5/2/2.5 & 38 & 289 & 176 & 0.61 \\
Mistral/Mixtral & 29 & 198 & 109 & 0.55 \\
\midrule
Weighted avg & 114 & --- & --- & 0.62 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The results are striking: 62\% of cross-cohort DIF flags also appear within families. In the Llama family, 68\% of flags that distinguish temporal cohorts also distinguish Llama-2 from Llama-3---models with known capability improvements but no known differential contamination. This means DIF is primarily measuring ``the model got better'' rather than ``the model saw the test data.''

External validation against MMLU-CF yields Cohen's $\kappa = 0.07$ (near chance), though this partly reflects MMLU-CF's own annotation inconsistency ($\kappa = 0.14$). Domain-specificity analysis finds no significant clustering of DIF flags across known contamination-prone vs.\ capability-growth domains ($\chi^2$ test, $p = 0.23$).

\paragraph{Item-Focused Trees as alternative.} We also implement Item-Focused Trees (IFT), which treat release date as a continuous covariate rather than requiring categorical cohort definitions (Table~\ref{tab:ift}).

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Item-Focused Trees vs.\ Categorical DIF. IFT provides marginal improvement but fundamentally cannot resolve the upstream IRT misfit problem. The 48\% flag overlap between methods further demonstrates instability.}
\label{tab:ift}
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Method} & \textbf{AUROC} & \textbf{TPR ($\delta \geq 1.4$)} & \textbf{FPR} & \textbf{Flag Count} \\
\midrule
Categorical temporal & 0.55 & 0.42 & 0.14 & 1,850 \\
Categorical quintile & 0.53 & 0.38 & 0.16 & 2,300 \\
Item-Focused Tree & 0.57 & 0.39 & 0.12 & 1,620 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

IFT achieves marginal AUROC improvement (0.57 vs.\ 0.55) with lower FPR (0.12 vs.\ 0.14) but overlaps only 48\% with categorical DIF flags. The cross-validation gap of 0.09 suggests overfitting, and the fundamental issue---IRT model misfit and capability confounding---is upstream of the cohort definition.

%% -------- 4.3 RL Robustness --------
\subsection{RL Robustness Analysis}
\label{sec:rl_robust}

We evaluate DIF alongside traditional detectors across three model training paradigms: SFT-only, alignment-RL (DPO/ORPO), and reasoning-RL (GRPO/PPO).

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Detection AUROC across RL training types. All detectors degrade under reasoning-RL. DIF degrades less steeply (retention ratio 0.71 vs.\ 0.65--0.66) but from a lower SFT baseline, resulting in near-random absolute performance on reasoning-RL models.}
\label{tab:rl_comparison}
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Detector} & \textbf{SFT AUROC} & \textbf{Align-RL} & \textbf{Reason-RL} & \textbf{Retention} \\
\midrule
DIF (ETS C) & 0.62 & 0.58 & 0.55 & 0.71 \\
ConStat & 0.73 & 0.61 & 0.48 & 0.66 \\
Min-K\% & 0.68 & 0.55 & 0.44 & 0.65 \\
Min-K\%++ & 0.71 & 0.59 & 0.46 & 0.65 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

DIF exhibits higher retention ratio (0.71) than alternatives (0.65--0.66), nominally supporting the theoretical argument that group-level behavioral signals are more robust to RL smoothing. However, this is a pyrrhic victory: DIF's SFT baseline AUROC is already only 0.62 (vs.\ ConStat's 0.73), so ``retaining'' 71\% of a weak signal yields 0.55 AUROC on reasoning-RL---barely above chance. Bootstrap CI for reasoning-RL AUROC spans [0.48, 0.62], crossing the random baseline. With only $N=12$ reasoning-RL models available, statistical power is insufficient for definitive claims.

\paragraph{Partial exploitation.} Real-world contamination may be partial: models exploit memorized items with probability $p < 1$. Table~\ref{tab:partial_exploit} characterizes detection power as a function of exploitation rate.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Detection power under partial exploitation. Even at full exploitation ($p=1.0$), real-data TPR is 0.42---far below the 0.72 simulation prediction. At realistic exploitation rates ($p \leq 0.6$), detection is effectively impossible.}
\label{tab:partial_exploit}
\begin{tabular}{ccccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Exploit.\ Rate $p$} & \textbf{DIF TPR} & \textbf{DIF FPR} & \textbf{Ens.\ TPR} & \textbf{Ens.\ FPR} \\
\midrule
1.0 & 0.42 & 0.14 & 0.48 & 0.16 \\
0.8 & 0.33 & 0.13 & 0.41 & 0.15 \\
0.6 & 0.24 & 0.12 & 0.38 & 0.14 \\
0.4 & 0.15 & 0.11 & 0.28 & 0.13 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

At full exploitation ($p=1.0$), real-data TPR is 0.42---a 43\% gap from simulation-predicted 0.72. At $p=0.6$, TPR drops to 0.24, and at $p=0.4$, detection is near-random (TPR\,=\,0.15 vs.\ FPR\,=\,0.11).

%% -------- 4.4 Alternative Detectors --------
\subsection{Alternative Detection Paradigms}
\label{sec:alt_detect}

\subsubsection{ConStat Perturbation Testing}

We generate 220 semantically equivalent reformulations of MMLU items (mean semantic similarity 0.87) and measure contamination signals via performance degradation on reformulated items.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{ConStat detection power by model type. Performance degrades sharply for RL-trained models, with reasoning-RL AUROC falling below chance (signal inversion).}
\label{tab:constat}
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Model Type} & $N$ & \textbf{AUROC} & \textbf{FDR-TPR ($\alpha{=}0.05$)} & \textbf{FDR-TPR ($\alpha{=}0.10$)} \\
\midrule
SFT-only & 85 & 0.62 & 0.34 & 0.42 \\
Align-RL (DPO/ORPO) & 65 & 0.51 & 0.22 & 0.31 \\
Reason-RL (GRPO) & 12 & 0.43 & 0.15 & 0.24 \\
All & 162 & 0.54 & 0.28 & 0.36 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Overall AUROC is 0.54, far below previously reported performance. ConStat fails for two reasons. First, perturbation inevitably alters difficulty (mean shift 0.35 logits, Table~\ref{tab:perturb_quality}), confounding contamination signals with difficulty artifacts. Second, RL training produces output distribution smoothing that \emph{reduces} perturbation sensitivity even for non-contaminated items---on reasoning-RL models, AUROC falls to 0.43 (below chance), indicating signal inversion.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Perturbation quality assessment. Despite high semantic similarity, reformulations introduce non-trivial difficulty shifts that confound contamination detection.}
\label{tab:perturb_quality}
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Metric} & \textbf{Mean} & \textbf{Std} & \textbf{Min} & \textbf{Max} \\
\midrule
Semantic similarity & 0.87 & 0.06 & 0.71 & 0.97 \\
Difficulty shift (logit) & 0.35 & 0.41 & $-$0.52 & 1.83 \\
Distractor attractiveness $\Delta$ & 0.22 & 0.31 & $-$0.18 & 1.12 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsubsection{DVD Generation Variance}

We evaluate generation-variance-based detection using 50 temperature-sampled responses per item across 13 models and 500 items.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{DVD detection power. The compressed output space of multiple-choice questions fundamentally limits variance-based discrimination.}
\label{tab:dvd}
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Contamination Type} & \textbf{TPR} & \textbf{FPR} & \textbf{AUROC} & $N_\text{items}$ \\
\midrule
Verbatim & 0.31 & 0.14 & 0.62 & 150 \\
Variant & 0.23 & 0.14 & 0.56 & 150 \\
Overall & 0.27 & 0.14 & 0.58 & 300 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Generation variance distributions for contaminated vs.\ clean items. Cohen's $d = 0.30$ indicates a small effect, and 72\% distribution overlap renders variance an unreliable discriminator.}
\label{tab:dvd_dist}
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Item Status} & \textbf{Var.\ Mean} & \textbf{Var.\ Std} & \textbf{Bimodal Rate} \\
\midrule
Contaminated & 0.34 & 0.18 & 0.18 \\
Clean & 0.29 & 0.16 & 0.11 \\
\midrule
Effect size $d$ & 0.30 & --- & --- \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

DVD achieves overall AUROC\,=\,0.58 with effect size $d = 0.30$ (small). The fundamental limitation is structural: MMLU's multiple-choice format compresses the output space to four options, severely limiting variance-based discrimination. Contaminated and clean item variance distributions overlap 72\%. DVD's bimodal detection rate difference (0.18 vs.\ 0.11) is insufficient for reliable item-level classification.

%% -------- 4.5 Ensemble Fusion --------
\subsection{Ensemble Fusion}
\label{sec:ensemble}

Given that DIF, ConStat, and DVD capture different aspects of contamination (low inter-signal correlations, Table~\ref{tab:signal_corr}), we evaluate whether ensemble fusion can aggregate weak signals into reliable detection.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Ensemble fusion strategy comparison. Weighted fusion achieves the highest TPR but at elevated FPR, and even the best ensemble detects fewer than half of large-effect contaminated items.}
\label{tab:ensemble}
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Strategy} & \textbf{TPR ($\delta \geq 0.8$)} & \textbf{TPR ($\delta \geq 1.4$)} & \textbf{FPR} & \textbf{F1} \\
\midrule
DIF only & 0.28 & 0.42 & 0.14 & 0.33 \\
ConStat only & 0.31 & 0.36 & 0.12 & 0.34 \\
DVD only & 0.20 & 0.27 & 0.14 & 0.24 \\
Majority (2-of-3) & 0.32 & 0.40 & 0.11 & 0.37 \\
Weighted fusion & 0.43 & 0.48 & 0.16 & 0.40 \\
Uniform weight & 0.39 & 0.45 & 0.14 & 0.39 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Inter-signal correlation matrix. Low correlations indicate signals capture different facets, but in this regime low correlation reflects distinct noise sources rather than complementary signals.}
\label{tab:signal_corr}
\begin{tabular}{lcc}
\toprule
\textbf{Signal Pair} & \textbf{Pearson $r$} & \textbf{Flag Overlap} \\
\midrule
DIF--ConStat & 0.22 & 0.28 \\
DIF--DVD & 0.14 & 0.19 \\
ConStat--DVD & 0.18 & 0.23 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Weighted fusion achieves the best TPR at $\delta \geq 1.4$ (0.48) but at the cost of FPR\,=\,0.16, producing approximately 2,000 false alarms on MMLU's 12,508 items. Cross-validation weight stability is poor (CV\,=\,0.35), indicating overfitting risk. Each signal's unique contribution is small (DIF: 8\%, ConStat: 11\%, DVD: 5\%), suggesting the overlap region represents shared noise rather than reinforced signal. Three weak detectors do not produce a strong ensemble.

%% -------- 4.6 Cross-Benchmark Validation --------
\subsection{Cross-Benchmark Validation: GSM8K}
\label{sec:gsm8k}

To test generalizability, we replicate DIF analysis on GSM8K (1,319 items, 58 models) and validate against the independent GSM1K ground truth.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{GSM8K cross-validation. DIF flags show no statistically significant correlation with the GSM8K--GSM1K accuracy gap, and the 95\% CI for $r$ includes zero.}
\label{tab:gsm8k}
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Metric} & \textbf{Value} & \textbf{95\% CI Lower} & \textbf{95\% CI Upper} \\
\midrule
DIF--gap correlation $r$ & 0.14 & $-$0.12 & 0.38 \\
DIF TPR vs.\ GSM1K & 0.22 & 0.10 & 0.36 \\
DIF FPR vs.\ GSM1K & 0.09 & 0.04 & 0.16 \\
IRT RMSEA & 0.10 & 0.08 & 0.12 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The DIF--gap correlation of $r = 0.14$ ($p = 0.32$) is not statistically significant, with the 95\% CI spanning zero. DIF TPR against the GSM1K proxy is only 0.22. IRT fit is better on GSM8K (RMSEA\,=\,0.10) than MMLU, consistent with mathematical reasoning being more unidimensional, but still in the borderline range. The failure reflects a granularity mismatch: the GSM8K--GSM1K gap is a model-level aggregate, while DIF flags are item-level---their alignment requires the strong assumption that contamination is uniform across items.

%% -------- 4.7 Semi-Synthetic Validation --------
\subsection{Semi-Synthetic Contamination Validation}
\label{sec:semi_synth}

Our semi-synthetic framework injects contamination of known magnitude into the real METABENCH response matrix, providing the controlled ground truth that neither MMLU-CF ($\kappa = 0.07$) nor GSM1K ($r = 0.14$) can offer.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Semi-synthetic detection power across contamination parameters. Even under the most favorable conditions ($\delta = 1.4$, full exploitation), TPR is only 0.41.}
\label{tab:semi_synth}
\begin{tabular}{cccccc}
\toprule
$\delta$ & \textbf{Prevalence} & \textbf{Exploit.\ Rate} & \textbf{TPR} & \textbf{FPR} & \textbf{AUROC} \\
\midrule
1.4 & 0.15 & 1.0 & 0.41 & 0.14 & 0.68 \\
1.4 & 0.15 & 0.6 & 0.24 & 0.13 & 0.59 \\
1.0 & 0.15 & 1.0 & 0.28 & 0.14 & 0.60 \\
1.0 & 0.15 & 0.6 & 0.17 & 0.13 & 0.54 \\
0.6 & 0.15 & 1.0 & 0.15 & 0.14 & 0.52 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Simulation-to-reality gap. Real response matrix noise structures cause a consistent $\sim$43\% reduction in detection power relative to idealized simulations, quantifying the fundamental limitation of simulation-based power analysis.}
\label{tab:sim_gap}
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Condition} & \textbf{Sim TPR} & \textbf{Semi-Syn TPR} & \textbf{Gap} & \textbf{Gap \%} \\
\midrule
$N{=}100$, $\delta{=}1.4$, $p{=}1.0$ & 0.72 & 0.41 & 0.31 & 43\% \\
$N{=}100$, $\delta{=}1.0$, $p{=}1.0$ & 0.52 & 0.28 & 0.24 & 46\% \\
$N{=}80$, $\delta{=}1.4$, $p{=}1.0$ & 0.68 & 0.38 & 0.30 & 44\% \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Focal group size effect on detection power ($\delta = 1.4$). Power increases with group size but with sharply diminishing returns---reaching 0.47 at $N=200$ but still below the 0.80 conventional threshold.}
\label{tab:focal_size}
\begin{tabular}{cccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Focal $N$} & \textbf{TPR ($\delta{=}1.4$)} & \textbf{FPR} & \textbf{Power Gain / 10 Models} \\
\midrule
40 & 0.29 & 0.15 & --- \\
60 & 0.34 & 0.14 & 0.025 \\
80 & 0.38 & 0.14 & 0.020 \\
100 & 0.41 & 0.14 & 0.015 \\
200 & 0.47 & 0.13 & 0.006 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The simulation-to-reality gap is remarkably consistent at $\sim$43--46\% across conditions. Three noise sources in real response data drive this gap: (1) non-random missingness correlated with model capability, biasing IRT parameter estimation; (2) ceiling/floor effects on easy/hard items eliminating ability-conditional variation; (3) architecture clustering violating the independence assumption. Power scales sub-linearly with focal group size (diminishing returns above $N=100$), suggesting that simply collecting more models will not solve the problem.

Prevalence shows a counter-intuitive negative relationship: TPR at 5\% prevalence (0.48) exceeds 15\% (0.39) and 30\% (0.33), because high prevalence dilutes the focal--reference contrast that DIF requires.

%% -------- 4.8 Bifactor IRT & Equating --------
\subsection{Bifactor IRT and Test Equating}
\label{sec:bifactor}

We attempt bifactor IRT modeling and anchor-item-based equating as a foundation for psychometrically principled benchmark regeneration.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Bifactor model fit. The full 57-subject model fails to converge. Subset models converge with borderline fit, but the general factor explains only 38\% of variance, indicating high multidimensionality.}
\label{tab:bifactor}
\begin{tabular}{lccccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Scope} & \textbf{Converged} & \textbf{RMSEA} & \textbf{Gen.\ Var.} & \textbf{Subj.\ Var.} & \textbf{Resid.\ Var.} \\
\midrule
Full (57 subjects) & No & --- & --- & --- & --- \\
Subset (8 subjects) & Yes & 0.09 & 0.38 & 0.31 & 0.31 \\
Subset (4 STEM) & Yes & 0.07 & 0.44 & 0.28 & 0.28 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The full 57-subject bifactor model does not converge, due to the parameter count ($2 \times 12{,}508$ item parameters + 57 factor loadings) exceeding the effective information in the sparse response matrix. Restricting to 8 subjects achieves convergence with RMSEA\,=\,0.09, but the general factor explains only 38\% of variance---over 60\% is domain-specific or residual, confirming that LLM ability is highly multidimensional and a single equating framework is inappropriate.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Equating quality metrics. All targets are unmet: too few anchor items, unstable parameters, and unacceptable rank displacement for leaderboard applications.}
\label{tab:equating}
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Metric} & \textbf{Value} & \textbf{Target} & \textbf{Met} \\
\midrule
Anchor items & 145 & $>$500 & No \\
Anchor stability rate & 0.12 & $>$0.80 & No \\
Spearman $\rho$ & 0.84 & $>$0.95 & No \\
Mean rank displacement & 23 & $<$5 & No \\
Fixed-param convergence & 0.71 & $>$0.90 & No \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Equating fails on every quality criterion: only 145 anchor items (vs.\ 500+ needed), 12\% stability rate (vs.\ 80\% target), and mean rank displacement of 23 positions. The last metric means approximately 5\% of models would shift by more than one percentile in an equated leaderboard---unacceptable for evaluation purposes.

%% -------- 4.9 Item Generation --------
\subsection{Item Generation and Calibration}
\label{sec:item_gen}

We evaluate whether LLM-generated replacement items can achieve psychometric equivalence with original items.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Item generation calibration quality. Student simulation substantially outperforms prompt-based calibration but still falls short of targets for psychometric equivalence.}
\label{tab:item_gen}
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Calibration Method} & \textbf{Difficulty $\rho$} & \textbf{Discrim.\ $\rho$} & \textbf{Construct Loading} \\
\midrule
Prompt-based & 0.21 & 0.14 & 0.48 \\
Student simulation & 0.58 & 0.41 & 0.52 \\
Target & $>$0.75 & $>$0.60 & $>$0.70 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Prompt-based difficulty targeting performs poorly ($\rho = 0.21$), consistent with prior findings on the disconnect between LLM difficulty perception and actual psychometric difficulty. Student simulation---using a calibration panel of diverse models as simulated examinees---improves difficulty correlation to $\rho = 0.58$ and discrimination to $\rho = 0.41$, a meaningful advance but still below the 0.75 and 0.60 thresholds needed for psychometric equivalence.

The critical failure is construct validity: generated items achieve mean factor loading of 0.52 vs.\ the 0.70 target. Items are topically aligned with originals but measure subtly different constructs. Only 34\% of generated items pass all acceptance criteria (difficulty, discrimination, and construct loading), meaning 300 candidates are needed to replace 100 flagged items---a 3:1 generation cost.

%% -------- 4.10 Cumulative Bayesian DIF --------
\subsection{Cumulative Bayesian DIF}
\label{sec:bayesian}

We implement cumulative Bayesian DIF priors following Sinharay \etal~(2006), testing whether accumulating evidence across successive model cohort ``administrations'' can rescue detection power.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Cumulative Bayesian DIF power. Accumulation doubles power over 4 administrations, but the absolute level remains below the 0.80 threshold. The simulation-to-reality ratio worsens over time, suggesting prior accumulation amplifies systematic bias in real data.}
\label{tab:bayesian}
\begin{tabular}{cccc}
\toprule
$N_\text{admin}$ & \textbf{Power (Real)} & \textbf{Power (Sim)} & \textbf{Ratio} \\
\midrule
1 & 0.22 & 0.40 & 0.55 \\
2 & 0.31 & 0.54 & 0.57 \\
3 & 0.39 & 0.64 & 0.61 \\
4 & 0.45 & 0.70 & 0.64 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Cumulative evidence yields a 2.05$\times$ power improvement over 4 administrations (0.22\,$\to$\,0.45), but the absolute power level (0.45) remains far below the 0.80 threshold for reliable detection. The simulation-to-reality ratio actually \emph{improves} from 0.55 to 0.64, suggesting accumulation partially compensates for real-data noise. However, this assumes item parameters remain stable across administrations---an assumption violated by the evolving LLM capability landscape. The Bayesian prior may accumulate outdated information rather than strengthening signals.

%% -------- 4.11 Graceful Degradation --------
\subsection{Graceful Degradation Analysis}
\label{sec:degradation}

Given the limitations of both detection and regeneration, we characterize how benchmark validity degrades as contaminated items are replaced with imperfect substitutes.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Graceful degradation under item replacement. Given the low quality of replacement items (construct loading 0.52), validity degrades rapidly---the acceptable replacement threshold is only 5--10\%.}
\label{tab:degradation}
\begin{tabular}{cccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Replace Rate} & \textbf{Rank Stability} & \textbf{Score Reliability} & \textbf{Construct Coverage} \\
\midrule
5\% & 0.96 & 0.94 & 0.97 \\
10\% & 0.91 & 0.89 & 0.93 \\
20\% & 0.82 & 0.78 & 0.85 \\
50\% & 0.63 & 0.55 & 0.61 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

At 10\% replacement, ranking stability drops to 0.91 (below the 0.95 threshold), score reliability to 0.89, and construct coverage to 0.93. At 20\% replacement, the benchmark is substantially degraded. The acceptable threshold of 5--10\% creates a practical ceiling: if contamination affects more than 10\% of items (our DIF analysis flags 12--18\%), full regeneration is not feasible without higher-quality replacement items. At 50\% replacement, the result is effectively a different test (rank stability 0.63, reliability 0.55).

%% ============================================================
%% 5. DISCUSSION
%% ============================================================
\section{Discussion}

\paragraph{Why classical psychometrics fails for LLMs.}
The fundamental obstacle is that LLMs violate core IRT assumptions in ways human examinees do not. Human test-takers within a grade level are approximately homogeneous in their cognitive architecture; LLMs spanning encoder--decoder, dense, and MoE architectures are not. The resulting model misfit (RMSEA\,=\,0.14) corrupts all downstream psychometric analyses. Even within the most homogeneous subgroup (dense decoder-only, RMSEA\,=\,0.08), the within-family confound overlap of 0.47 means nearly half of detected DIF reflects capability growth rather than contamination.

\paragraph{The detection trilemma.}
Our results reveal a three-way trade-off: (1) adequate IRT fit requires homogeneous subpopulations, (2) statistical power requires large sample sizes, and (3) confound control requires known-clean within-family comparisons. No single analysis can simultaneously satisfy all three. Architecture stratification addresses (1) but sacrifices (2); within-family analysis addresses (3) but with small $N$; the full population maximizes (2) but violates (1).

\paragraph{Simulation overestimates real-world performance.}
The consistent $\sim$43\% simulation-to-reality gap is perhaps our most broadly applicable finding. Any contamination detection method validated solely through simulation should expect comparable degradation when applied to real response matrices. This gap arises from structural properties of real evaluation data (non-random missingness, ceiling/floor effects, architecture clustering) that are absent from standard simulation frameworks.

\paragraph{Methodological contributions.}
Despite the negative empirical results, we contribute three reusable methodological tools: (1) the confound-separation analysis via within-family DIF provides a template for distinguishing contamination from capability in any detection framework; (2) the semi-synthetic validation protocol offers controlled ground truth superior to existing proxies; (3) the graceful degradation framework quantifies the cost of imperfect item replacement for any regeneration approach.

\paragraph{Implications for the community.}
Our findings suggest that item-level contamination detection from behavioral response patterns alone may be fundamentally limited when the ``examinee'' population is architecturally heterogeneous and rapidly evolving. Future detection methods should either (a) incorporate training data access (moving beyond black-box detection), (b) exploit format-specific signals beyond binary correct/incorrect (\eg, chain-of-thought analysis), or (c) design benchmarks with built-in contamination resistance (\eg, procedurally generated items with known difficulty functions).

%% ============================================================
%% 6. CONCLUSION
%% ============================================================
\section{Conclusion}

We set out to build a psychometric contamination detection and benchmark regeneration framework, and instead produced the first large-scale empirical evidence for why this is harder than the community has assumed. Across 5,219 models and 12,508 MMLU items, we demonstrate that: (1) classical IRT assumptions are catastrophically violated in heterogeneous LLM populations; (2) DIF-based contamination signals are inseparable from capability growth signals, with 62\% confound overlap; (3) all existing detection paradigms---DIF, perturbation testing, generation variance, and their ensemble---fail to achieve reliable detection, with the best configuration reaching 0.48 TPR at $\delta \geq 1.4$; (4) the $\sim$43\% simulation-to-reality gap means published power analyses substantially overestimate real-world performance; and (5) bifactor equating and item regeneration are not yet feasible at scale.

These negative results are themselves a contribution: they set realistic expectations for contamination detection, provide diagnostic tools for understanding detection failures, and offer a semi-synthetic validation framework for evaluating future approaches. The path forward likely requires moving beyond behavioral response patterns to richer signal sources---or fundamentally rethinking how we design benchmarks for an era where the distinction between ``learning'' and ``memorizing'' is increasingly blurred.

